From aedf15c8c438b5aec84d8862228c95b0030c166c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 17:22:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] android: EGL / OpenGL ES presentation backend
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds Broiler.Graphics.Android, the Android half of the presentation layer, plus
the Android system-font paths the core fallback was missing.

The backend is small for a reason that is worth stating: Broiler already
rasterizes every frame on the CPU through BImageRenderer on every platform, and
the GPU backends only upload that frame as a texture and blit it. There is no
shader pipeline, no vertex data, and no draw call here either — so Android
inherits Broiler's rendering behavior unchanged and adds no new layout, paint, or
conformance surface. What is Android-specific is presentation, not rendering.

Three things could not be copied from the Linux EGL backend, and each is a hard
failure if missed. Android has no desktop GL, so the context binds
EGL_OPENGL_ES_API (0x30A0) and the config asks for EGL_OPENGL_ES3_BIT (0x40)
rather than EGL_OPENGL_API and EGL_OPENGL_BIT. The library soname is libEGL.so
with no .1 suffix. And glBlitFramebuffer is an ES 3.0 entry point, which fixes
the feature floor: an ES 2 context would load and then fail at present time, so
context creation refuses anything below ES 3 to turn that into a clear creation
error.

The surface lifecycle is the part with no Linux equivalent. On a handheld the
drawing surface is destroyed and recreated constantly — every rotation, every
backgrounding — while the context and its GPU resources can survive. So the
display, config, and context live for the session and only the EGLSurface is torn
down and rebuilt, through AttachNativeWindow and DetachNativeWindow driven by the
host's SurfaceHolder callbacks. Rebuilding the context on every rotation would
discard the texture and framebuffer for nothing. A frame that arrives while
detached is retained on the CPU and reported in Diagnostic rather than throwing,
because frames do arrive mid-rotation and taking the Activity down for it would
be a bug. A context that genuinely dies reports EGL_CONTEXT_LOST, which surfaces
as the neutral BDeviceLostException carrying the EGL code, so a host recovers the
same way it would from a Direct3D device reset.

Like the input backends, this targets plain net10.0 and reaches Android through
P/Invoke only. That is possible because ANativeWindow_fromSurface takes a JNIEnv*
and a jobject and both are plain pointers, so a host passes
JniEnvironment.EnvironmentPointer and surface.Handle and no Android managed type
crosses into Graphics. A boundary test pins the absence of those references.

FallbackSystemFont knew only Linux, Windows, and macOS paths, so an Android build
would have found no font at all and rendered no text. /system/fonts is now a font
root and Roboto, Noto, and Droid are candidate pairs.

16 tests cover geometry, pixel orientation, the EGL constants that differ from
desktop, the ES3-only import surface, the dependency probe, and the detached
surface state machine. They deliberately stop at the native boundary: creating a
context, uploading, blitting, and reading back all need a real EGL implementation
and belong to the phase A1 hardware gate. Nothing here has run on a device.

Graphics core suite still passes 56/56 with the font change, and the Linux
browser and writer solutions still build.
